    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-107560",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine",
      "problem": "Given that the expression $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-ϕ)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R?$",
      "worked_solution": "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =1+2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =3 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$",
