    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-107572",
      "knowledge_point_id": "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine",
      "problem": "$6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$",
      "worked_solution": "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=6 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{8}{6} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ ϕ & ≈0.927\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ & ={6}^{2}+{8}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =36+64 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =100 \\\\ R & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.927)\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
